            },
            "kind": "REWORK",
            "comments": [
                {
                    "file": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
                    "line": 0,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Joseph Reynolds",
                        "email": "joseph.reynolds1@ibm.com",
                        "username": "joseph-reynolds"
                    },
                    "message": "I wonder if Yocto/OE would be interesting in taking this patch..."
                },
                {
                    "file": "meta-phosphor/recipes-connectivity/openssh/openssh/add-sshd-account-if-needed.sh",
                    "line": 0,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "nit: can we not add \".sh\" to scripts?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "meta-phosphor/recipes-connectivity/openssh/openssh/add-sshd-account-if-needed.sh",
                    "line": 0,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Joseph Reynolds",
                        "email": "joseph.reynolds1@ibm.com",
                        "username": "joseph-reynolds"
                    },
                    "message": "Yes.  I'll remove the \".sh\" suffix.  I did not realize the preference for no suffix."
                },
                {
                    "file": "meta-phosphor/recipes-connectivity/openssh/openssh_%.bbappend",
                    "line": 6,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "Why does an sshd account _need_ to be created?  I don't see anything in the systemd socket (or service) file that leverage that account.  Is sshd process (when launched from the socket file) itself downgrading to it?  What happens if the account doesn't exist?\n\nAll of the systems in meta-facebook use sshd already and they have the sshd account created by default when building them.  But... I don't see that account ever used.\n\nThis was from the serial console of a QEMU session of \"catalina\" while ssh-ing.\n```\nroot@catalina:~# ps | grep ssh\n 1485 root      7704 S    sshd-session: root [priv]\n 1490 root      7964 S    sshd-session: root@pts/0\n 1499 root      2828 S    grep ssh\n```"
                },
                {
                    "file": "meta-phosphor/recipes-connectivity/openssh/openssh_%.bbappend",
                    "line": 6,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Joseph Reynolds",
                        "email": "joseph.reynolds1@ibm.com",
                        "username": "joseph-reynolds"
                    },
                    "message": "See https://github.com/openssh/openssh-portable/blob/master/README.privsep\nPrivilege separation, which requires a sshd system account, apparently became mandatory in 2019.  I understand the sshd server (which necessarily runs as root) drops into (becomes) the sshd user for operations which do not require root authority, specifically during pre-authentication."
                },
                {
                    "file": "meta-phosphor/recipes-connectivity/openssh/openssh_%.bbappend",
                    "line": 6,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "I'm somewhat fine if you want to enable this all the time then if you think it really is necessary.  But, I'm fine if we want to keep it as a PACKAGECONFIG also.\n\nThere probably aren't many people that are going to actively \"upgrade\" from Dropbear to OpenSSH after they've already released a product, so this workflow likely isn't really needed in most cases.  OpenSSH creates the account by default (in Yocto) and has since before OpenBMC existed.\n\nhttps://github.com/openembedded/openembedded-core/commit/6b7f399d595ef58e759dab211f4ece155119a680"
                },
                {
                    "file": "meta-phosphor/recipes-connectivity/openssh/openssh_%.bbappend",
                    "line": 6,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Joseph Reynolds",
                        "email": "joseph.reynolds1@ibm.com",
                        "username": "joseph-reynolds"
                    },
                    "message": "I agree; I want to switch our production systems from dropbear to openssh only at a hard version boundary (meaning, no production system update path from firmware running dropbear to firmware running openssh), so this service will not be needed on production systems.  This addresses a \"corner case\" that comes up mostly in our development systems which transition from dropbear to openssh.  I would prefer to keep this as a disabled-by-default PACKAGECONFIG to ease migrations from dropbear to openssh."
                },
                {
                    "file": "meta-phosphor/recipes-connectivity/openssh/openssh_%.bbappend",
                    "line": 6,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "Acknowledged"
                },
                {
                    "file": "meta-phosphor/recipes-connectivity/openssh/openssh_%.bbappend",
                    "line": 31,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "Let's not add this in sbin.  libexec maybe.  There is no reason to make this available in the PATH."
                },
                {
                    "file": "meta-phosphor/recipes-connectivity/openssh/openssh_%.bbappend",
                    "line": 31,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Joseph Reynolds",
                        "email": "joseph.reynolds1@ibm.com",
                        "username": "joseph-reynolds"
                    },
                    "message": "Good idea.  I was unaware of this directory.  I'll make the change."
                },
                {
                    "file": "meta-phosphor/recipes-connectivity/openssh/openssh_%.bbappend",
                    "line": 31,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "https://github.com/openbmc/docs/blob/master/anti-patterns.md#incorrect-placement-of-executables-in-sbin-usrsbin-or-bin-usrbin"
                }
            ],
